I've gone through the whole project — structure, auth, data layer, security, admin, and the git state. Here's the complete picture.

# Correct Student — project walkthrough

## What this is

**Correct Student** (`correct-student` in package.json) is a language-learning platform for African languages — **Yoruba, Igbo, Hausa, Fulani** — built on **Next.js 16 (App Router)** with **React 19**, **Tailwind v4 + shadcn-style UI**, **Drizzle ORM on PostgreSQL**, and **Better Auth** for authentication. It's a Nigerian product (prices in ₦ with USD/GBP alternates). The entire application is currently **uncommitted** on top of the single initial `create-next-app` commit — ~205 changed/new files.

## Architecture

**Route groups** (`app/`):
- **`(marketing)/`** — public site: home, about, classes + class detail, shop + product detail, blog + post detail, pricing, FAQs, contact, privacy, terms; auth pages (sign-in, sign-up, get-started) with Navbar/Footer chrome.
- **`(dashboard)/`** — three role-scoped workspaces with shared `DashboardShell` sidebar layout:
  - **`/admin`** — ~17 sections: overview, newsletter, contact, languages, subscriptions, students, tutors, classes, blog, products, orders, coupons, activities, content-types, media-types, levels, media, settings — each with a list page and a `[record]` detail page (edit + delete-confirmation dialog).
  - **`/students`** — dashboard, explore classes, my learning, subscriptions, shop, saved, orders, settings.
  - **`/tutors`** — dashboard, my classes, students, schedule, assignments, messages, resources, settings.
- **`app/api/auth/[...all]`** — the only route handler; everything else uses **server actions**.

**Data layer** (`lib/`):
- `lib/schema.ts` — Drizzle schema: Better Auth tables (`user`, `session`, `account`, `verification`) plus `newsletter_subscriptions`, `contact_messages`, `rate_limits`, and content-managed `languages`, `content_types`, `media_types`, `levels`, `media`. `user` carries role (`student | tutor | admin | super-admin`) and `subscription` (`free | starter | plus | premium`) as DB enums.
- `lib/data/dal/*` + `lib/data/actions/*` — a clean DAL/server-action split, e.g. `languages` has full CRUD actions gated by `requireRole(["admin", "super-admin"])` and validated with zod schemas in `validation/`.
- `drizzle/` — 5 migrations (latest `0004_add_languages`), `drizzle.config.ts` reads `DATABASE_URL`.

## Auth & security (the strongest part of the codebase)

- **Better Auth** with `username` (immutable, regex-validated) and `admin` plugins; session **cookie cache (JWT, 5 min)**.
- **Closed role model** in [lib/auth/permissions.ts](lib/auth/permissions.ts): public signup can only pick **student/tutor** (enforced in a `databaseHooks.user.create.before` hook that overrides whatever the client submits — never trusts form fields); `subscription` is server-assigned only (`input: false`), always `free` at signup; only super-admin can create admins or change roles (admin role deliberately lacks `set-role`).
- **`proxy.ts`** (Next 16's renamed middleware) is optimistic cookie-only routing; **`lib/auth/session.ts`** does the real work: `getSession()` reads the cookie cache, treats banned users as unauthenticated, and `requireRole()` redirects wrong roles to `/redirect` — which maps each role to its dashboard home. Every dashboard layout wraps its content in a `RoleGate` under `Suspense` so a wrong-role request aborts before gated content renders.
- **Server actions** (`lib/auth/actions.ts`) — super-admin-only create admin / set role; admin+ ban/unban — all re-check role server-side.
- **Rate limiting on Postgres** ([lib/security/ratelimit.ts](lib/security/ratelimit.ts)): atomic upsert with SQL-driven window reset, `newsletter` 5/hr, `contact` 3/hr, plus login/register/etc. limits. Fails **closed** when IP detection is unavailable; `clientIp` trusts `x-forwarded-for` only on Vercel and `x-real-ip` only when `TRUST_PROXY_IP_HEADERS=true` (VPS). IP is SHA-256 hashed before storage.
- **Honeypots** on both newsletter (`newsletter_profile_website`) and contact forms with success-shaped fake responses.

## What's real vs. demo

- **Real, DB-backed:** auth, admin CRUD for languages/content-types/media-types/levels/media (media just stores Cloudinary URLs — provider integration is pending), newsletter subscriptions (with admin CSV export), contact messages (read/unread admin view with unread count).
- **Demo data, not wired to DB:** classes and shop products ([lib/catalog.ts](lib/catalog.ts)), blog posts ([lib/blog.ts](lib/blog.ts)), pricing plans ([lib/pricing.ts](lib/pricing.ts)), and the entire student/tutor workspace content ([lib/workspace-data.ts](lib/workspace-data.ts)) — student dashboard is hardcoded to "Amaka Obi" even for the signed-in user.
- **Not built yet:** payments (paid plans exist in UI/pricing only), Cloudinary integration for media, admin classes/blog/products as DB records.

## Build scripts & config

- `npm run dev / build / start / lint`. Seed scripts: `scripts/seed-super-admin.mjs <email> <name> <username> <password>` (creates or promotes a super-admin using better-auth's own hash) and `scripts/seed-content.ts` (inserts the 3 content types, 3 media types, 3 levels, 4 languages). Note: [scripts/seed-content.ts](scripts/seed-content.ts) has a duplicated `main().catch(...)` block — harmless but should be deduped.
- `next.config.ts`: `cacheComponents: true`, `partialPrefetching`, Tailwind via Turbopack, plus experimental agent settings. Pages that must read session/DB mark `export const instant = false`.
- `README.md` is now project-specific (newsletter, contact, rate-limit, VPS proxy notes) — and it's right that it flags the old gap; note auth **is now built**, so the README's "auth is not built yet" warning is stale.

## Notable observations / loose ends

1. **Big uncommitted surface** — the entire app is uncommitted; worth a commit soon.
2. **Admin data split-brain** — [lib/admin-data.ts](lib/admin-data.ts) still serves students/tutors/classes/orders/etc. from in-file demo arrays, while languages/contact/newsletter/media read the real DB. Those demo sections will need DB wiring as CRUD flows land.
3. **README staleness** — auth and admin sections exist now; the "no auth" warning is outdated.
4. `/admin` itself (index page) redirects to `/admin/dashboard`; admin-only `Staff` nav item appears only for super-admins — consistent with the role model.

The codebase is in good shape overall: consistent patterns (DAL → action → gate → revalidate), a genuinely careful auth design, and a clear separation between finished features and intentional placeholders.

There's still plenty of room to move from here — here are a few directions:

